   "quote": "reveal and showcase character within and through action scenes"
  },
  {
   "feature": "real people kept off the page",
   "claim": "Kay prefers a Valerius and Alixana based on Justinian and Theodora as an up-front admission that no one knows what the real people were like in private.",
   "source": "Teresa Basinski Eckford, 'Parallel Universes: Guy Gavriel Kay reflects on twenty years of alternative history', Historical Novels Review (Nov 2004)",
   "url": "https://brightweavings.com/hsparalleluniverses/",
   "quote": "we have no idea what the ‘real’ people were like"
  },
  {
   "feature": "variations on historical themes",
   "claim": "Kay describes his world-building as variations on historical themes rather than create-from-scratch worlds.",
   "source": "Teresa Basinski Eckford, 'Parallel Universes: Guy Gavriel Kay reflects on twenty years of alternative history', Historical Novels Review (Nov 2004)",
   "url": "https://brightweavings.com/hsparalleluniverses/",
   "quote": "variations on historical themes, not create-from-scratch worlds"
  },
  {
   "feature": "poet first",
   "claim": "Kay notes his first published work and awards were for poetry.",
   "source": "Teresa Basinski Eckford, 'Parallel Universes: Guy Gavriel Kay reflects on twenty years of alternative history', Historical Novels Review (Nov 2004)",
   "url": "https://brightweavings.com/hsparalleluniverses/",
   "quote": "My first published work was poetry"
  },
  {
   "feature": "same conversation retold differently",
   "claim": "Kay says several scenes in Children of Earth and Sky narrate one conversation from more than one participant and the conversation is not the same.",
   "source": "Christine Fischer Guy, 'The Fantastic Worlds of Guy Gavriel Kay', Los Angeles Review of Books (2016-08-14)",
   "url": "https://lareviewofbooks.org/article/fantastic-worlds-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "the conversation isn’t the same"
  },
  {
   "feature": "two moons as signal",
   "claim": "Kay says the two moons declare 'We're not here', setting the rules of engagement with the reader.",
   "source": "Christine Fischer Guy, 'The Fantastic Worlds of Guy Gavriel Kay', Los Angeles Review of Books (2016-08-14)",
   "url": "https://lareviewofbooks.org/article/fantastic-worlds-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "The two moons say, We’re not here."
  },
  {
   "feature": "borderland lives of the not-powerful",
   "claim": "Kay says Children of Earth and Sky takes place in the lives of not-powerful people on the borderlands for whom emperors' ambitions matter little.",
   "source": "Christine Fischer Guy, 'The Fantastic Worlds of Guy Gavriel Kay', Los Angeles Review of Books (2016-08-14)",
   "url": "https://lareviewofbooks.org/article/fantastic-worlds-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "in the lives of not-powerful people"
  },
  {
   "feature": "world as characters believe it",
   "claim": "Kay says the fantastic lets him present the world as characters believe it, matter-of-factly, removing the modern reader's smugness (his definition of magic realism).",
   "source": "Christine Fischer Guy, 'The Fantastic Worlds of Guy Gavriel Kay', Los Angeles Review of Books (2016-08-14)",
   "url": "https://lareviewofbooks.org/article/fantastic-worlds-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "the world is presented as the characters believe it to be"
  },
  {
   "feature": "past as alien, against relatability",
   "claim": "Kay calls the past staggeringly alien and criticises commercial historical writing that turns on relatability.",
   "source": "Christine Fischer Guy, 'The Fantastic Worlds of Guy Gavriel Kay', Los Angeles Review of Books (2016-08-14)",
   "url": "https://lareviewofbooks.org/article/fantastic-worlds-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "staggeringly alien"
  },
  {
   "feature": "invisible seam between fact and invention",
   "claim": "Kay says if a reader can pick out what is made up from what is real, the storytelling is botched.",
   "source": "Christine Fischer Guy, 'The Fantastic Worlds of Guy Gavriel Kay', Los Angeles Review of Books (2016-08-14)",
   "url": "https://lareviewofbooks.org/article/fantastic-worlds-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "you’ve botched your storytelling"
  },
  {
   "feature": "reverence for language / poetic prose",
   "claim": "The interviewer characterises Kay's prose as poetic and steeped in myth, with a reverence for language running through everything.",
   "source": "Christine Fischer Guy, 'The Fantastic Worlds of Guy Gavriel Kay', Los Angeles Review of Books (2016-08-14)",
   "url": "https://lareviewofbooks.org/article/fantastic-worlds-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "A reverence for language runs through everything he writes"
  },
  {
   "feature": "multiple points of view",
   "claim": "The interviewer states all Kay's novels are told from multiple points of view, some scenes from several characters in turn; Kay declines to call this a comment on who tells history.",
   "source": "Christine Fischer Guy, 'The Fantastic Worlds of Guy Gavriel Kay', Los Angeles Review of Books (2016-08-14)",
   "url": "https://lareviewofbooks.org/article/fantastic-worlds-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "Some scenes are told from several characters’ points of view"
  },
  {
   "feature": "quarter turn as shared admission",
   "claim": "Kay says the quarter turn acknowledges from the outset that we cannot get history exactly right, an awareness reader and author share.",
   "source": "Chris Urie, 'The Quarter Turn of History: A Conversation with Guy Gavriel Kay', Clarkesworld 117 (June 2016) — read via Wayback copy (live site is behind a JS challenge)",
   "url": "https://clarkesworldmagazine.com/kay_interview/",
   "quote": "we can’t get it exactly right"
  },
  {
   "feature": "rotating protagonists",
   "claim": "Kay says five protagonists all move to the centre in different parts of the narrative and balancing them is a core task.",
   "source": "Chris Urie, 'The Quarter Turn of History: A Conversation with Guy Gavriel Kay', Clarkesworld 117 (June 2016) — read via Wayback copy (live site is behind a JS challenge)",
   "url": "https://clarkesworldmagazine.com/kay_interview/",
   "quote": "all move to the center in different parts"
  },
  {
   "feature": "epic backdrop, ordinary lives",
   "claim": "Kay wanted a book that felt epic against a great war but was about non-powerful men and women getting on with their lives.",
   "source": "Chris Urie, 'The Quarter Turn of History: A Conversation with Guy Gavriel Kay', Clarkesworld 117 (June 2016) — read via Wayback copy (live site is behind a JS challenge)",
   "url": "https://clarkesworldmagazine.com/kay_interview/",
   "quote": "non-powerful men and women trying to get on"
  },
  {
   "feature": "no direct mapping",
   "claim": "Kay never wants a character or event to map directly onto a real one; history rhymes rather than repeats.",
   "source": "'Guy Gavriel Kay: Margins of History', Locus (May 2025 excerpt) — read via Wayback copy",
   "url": "https://locusmag.com/feature/guy-gavriel-kay-margins-of-history/",
   "quote": "where things map directly"
  },
  {
   "feature": "wit shown via unexplained references",
   "claim": "Kay borrows Dunnett's method of demonstrating intelligence by having characters say things the reader does not get.",
   "source": "'Guy Gavriel Kay: Margins of History', Locus (May 2025 excerpt) — read via Wayback copy",
   "url": "https://locusmag.com/feature/guy-gavriel-kay-margins-of-history/",
   "quote": "say things the reader doesn’t understand"
  },
  {
   "feature": "elements per book's need",
   "claim": "Kay lists the supernatural, the erotic, intrigue, suspense and lyric language as elements a book may or may not require.",
   "source": "'Guy Gavriel Kay: Margins of History', Locus (May 2025 excerpt) — read via Wayback copy",
   "url": "https://locusmag.com/feature/guy-gavriel-kay-margins-of-history/",
   "quote": "another might be lyric language"
  },
  {
   "feature": "hack verse as pastiche",
   "claim": "Kay enjoys writing a bad poet's work, e.g. the bad troubadour early in Arbonne.",
   "source": "'Guy Gavriel Kay: Margins of History', Locus (May 2025 excerpt) — read via Wayback copy",
   "url": "https://locusmag.com/feature/guy-gavriel-kay-margins-of-history/",
   "quote": "It’s fun writing a bad writer, too"
  },
  {
   "feature": "margins of history",
   "claim": "Kay says the unwritten lives of women and the poor at the margins of history have been part of his fiction forever.",
   "source": "'Guy Gavriel Kay: Margins of History', Locus (May 2025 excerpt) — read via Wayback copy",
   "url": "https://locusmag.com/feature/guy-gavriel-kay-margins-of-history/",
   "quote": "the people at the margins of history"
  },
  {
   "feature": "teaching the reader to read",
   "claim": "Kay says every serious writer is teaching a reader how to read them.",
   "source": "'Guy Gavriel Kay: Margins of History', Locus (May 2025 excerpt) — read via Wayback copy",
   "url": "https://locusmag.com/feature/guy-gavriel-kay-margins-of-history/",
   "quote": "teaching a reader how to read them"
  },
  {
   "feature": "cusp-of-transition settings",
   "claim": "Kay is drawn to times and places on the cusp of transition and to borderlands, while wary of proposing lessons.",
   "source": "'How Guy Gavriel Kay Mashes Up Real Events and People Into Alternate History Fantasy', Tor.com (2016-04-29; io9 interview digest) — read via Wayback copy",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/2016/04/29/guy-gavriel-kay-alternate-history-research",
   "quote": "times and places on the cusp of transition"
  },
  {
   "feature": "quarter turn imports other centuries",
   "claim": "Kay says the quarter turn let him give Children an emperor modelled on Rudolph II, a century out of period.",
   "source": "'How Guy Gavriel Kay Mashes Up Real Events and People Into Alternate History Fantasy', Tor.com (2016-04-29; io9 interview digest) — read via Wayback copy",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/2016/04/29/guy-gavriel-kay-alternate-history-research",
   "quote": "let me make use of some elements I couldn’t have"
  },
  {
   "feature": "no outlining",
   "claim": "Kay says he does not outline; period and place, then theme, then characters, and the arc emerges.",
   "source": "'A Conversation With Guy Gavriel Kay', Psychopomp.com (c. 2008)",
   "url": "https://psychopomp.com/fantasy/miscellaneous/a-conversation-with-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "I don’t outline the books."
  },
  {
   "feature": "Dunnett: character through action",
   "claim": "Kay praises Dunnett as unmatched at revealing character through action rather than stopping the narrative.",
   "source": "'A Conversation With Guy Gavriel Kay', Psychopomp.com (c. 2008)",
   "url": "https://psychopomp.com/fantasy/miscellaneous/a-conversation-with-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "revelation of character through action"
  },
  {
   "feature": "superlative-rich style (criticism)",
   "claim": "Harrison finds Kay's superlative-rich style risks beauty fatigue and that its extravagance might be better expressed as poems.",
   "source": "Niall Harrison, 'Under Heaven' (review), Vector / BSFA (2010-08-18)",
   "url": "https://vector-bsfa.com/2010/08/18/under-heaven/",
   "quote": "Kay’s superlative-rich style risks beauty fatigue"
  },
  {
   "feature": "magic but little mystery",
   "claim": "Harrison describes Kay's affect as magic but little mystery, a platonic ideal of Tang China described with precision and thoroughness.",
   "source": "Niall Harrison, 'Under Heaven' (review), Vector / BSFA (2010-08-18)",
   "url": "https://vector-bsfa.com/2010/08/18/under-heaven/",
   "quote": "magic but little mystery"
  },
  {
   "feature": "stately narrative",
   "claim": "Harrison says Tai's absurdly ambitious mourning is credible only when rendered within Kay's stately narrative.",
   "source": "Niall Harrison, 'Under Heaven' (review), Vector / BSFA (2010-08-18)",
   "url": "https://vector-bsfa.com/2010/08/18/under-heaven/",
   "quote": "rendered within Kay’s stately narrative"
  },
  {
   "feature": "implied narrator becomes real narrator",
   "claim": "Harrison notes that in the final third the implied narrator becomes a real narrator and the focus pulls back, making the novel a threnody and one telling among many.",
   "source": "Niall Harrison, 'Under Heaven' (review), Vector / BSFA (2010-08-18)",
   "url": "https://vector-bsfa.com/2010/08/18/under-heaven/",
   "quote": "the implied narrator becomes a real narrator"
  },
  {
   "feature": "set-piece events off-stage",
   "claim": "Harrison notes the horse-gift frame keeps expected big set-piece events off-stage in favour of the feel of a moment of historical possibility.",
   "source": "Niall Harrison, 'Under Heaven' (review), Vector / BSFA (2010-08-18)",
   "url": "https://vector-bsfa.com/2010/08/18/under-heaven/",
   "quote": "big set-piece events off-stage"
  },
  {
   "feature": "elevated register",
   "claim": "Harrison (in comments) says the elevated register is characteristic of Kay's work generally, not specific to Kitai.",
   "source": "Niall Harrison, 'Under Heaven' (review), Vector / BSFA (2010-08-18)",
   "url": "https://vector-bsfa.com/2010/08/18/under-heaven/",
   "quote": "the elevated register is characteristic of Kay’s work"
  },
  {
   "feature": "present tense for women's viewpoints",
   "claim": "Harrison (in comments) states Under Heaven's sections with women viewpoint characters are in present tense while male-viewpoint sections are in past tense.",
   "source": "Niall Harrison, 'Under Heaven' (review), Vector / BSFA (2010-08-18)",
   "url": "https://vector-bsfa.com/2010/08/18/under-heaven/",
   "quote": "are in present tense"
  },
  {
   "feature": "narrator on coincidence and historians",
   "claim": "Harrison quotes the narrator's remark that only a patient historian or a story-shaper would note such conjunctions, as Kay's self-reflexive use of history.",
   "source": "Niall Harrison, 'Under Heaven' (review), Vector / BSFA (2010-08-18)",
   "url": "https://vector-bsfa.com/2010/08/18/under-heaven/",
   "quote": "Only a patient historian with access to records"
  },
  {
   "feature": "veiled omniscient",
   "claim": "Walton names Kay's Sarantine style an odd, distanced, elegiac 'veiled omniscient': a narrator who knows everything but draws and lifts veils.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "odd, distanced, elegaic style that I want to call veiled omniscient"
  },
  {
   "feature": "withheld identity trick (criticism)",
   "claim": "Walton dislikes Kay's Dunnett-like trick of describing a character for pages without saying who is who.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "plays tricks where he described but doesn’t say who is who"
  },
  {
   "feature": "pulling back / artifice",
   "claim": "Walton says there is always a pulling back, events interpreted through a consciousness of artifice, like the wrong end of a telescope.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "there’s always a pulling back"
  },
  {
   "feature": "no throwaway viewpoints",
   "claim": "Walton notes many points of view but Kay never takes up a character just to throw them away; ironic omniscient linking underlines everything.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "he never takes up a character just to throw them away"
  },
  {
   "feature": "single-day compression and pluperfect",
   "claim": "Walton observes the books concentrate on single days with heavy flashback, using more pluperfect than anything she can think of, almost like Ulysses.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "more use of the pluperfect tense in these than anything else"
  },
  {
   "feature": "one event from many strata",
   "claim": "Walton notes a chariot race is seen from a driver, someone in the crowd and an undercook making soup.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "an undercook for the Blue faction making soup"
  },
  {
   "feature": "mediating metaphors",
   "claim": "Walton says Kay mediates the world through chariot races and mosaic-making, describing heresy and empire in those terms.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "mediates the world through the chariot races"
  },
  {
   "feature": "details that bite",
   "claim": "Walton praises details real enough to bite: tesserae quality, mud, fish sauces, the arrow-extraction tool.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "The details are all real enough to bite"
  },
  {
   "feature": "historical fantasy is open",
   "claim": "Walton argues renaming reshuffles the deck so no reader knows the ending: a historical novel is inevitably tragedy, a historical fantasy is open.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "a historical fantasy is open"
  },
  {
   "feature": "sparse glinting magic",
   "claim": "Walton says the little magic runs glinting through everything like silver threads in shot silk.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Mosaics and charioteers: Guy Gavriel Kay's Sarantine Mosaic', Tor.com/Reactor (2009-11-02)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/mosaics-and-charioteers-guy-gavriel-kays-sarantine-mosaic/",
   "quote": "like the silver threads in shot silk"
  },
  {
   "feature": "mannered and distanced (criticism)",
   "claim": "Walton finds Arbonne mannered, distanced and held at arm's length, its world denser than its gossamer characters.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Provencal Fantasy: Guy Gavriel Kay's A Song for Arbonne', Tor.com/Reactor (2010-08-06)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/provencal-fantasy-guy-gavriel-kays-a-song-for-arbonne/",
   "quote": "mannered and distanced and held at arm’s length"
  },
  {
   "feature": "set-piece moments",
   "claim": "Walton remembers Arbonne chiefly through set-piece moments of the kind Kay does so well.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Provencal Fantasy: Guy Gavriel Kay's A Song for Arbonne', Tor.com/Reactor (2010-08-06)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/provencal-fantasy-guy-gavriel-kays-a-song-for-arbonne/",
   "quote": "a few set-piece moments, of the kind Kay does so well"
  },
  {
   "feature": "trajectory away from magic",
   "claim": "Walton traces a trajectory away from magic from Fionavar through Tigana and Arbonne to Lions.",
   "source": "Jo Walton, 'Provencal Fantasy: Guy Gavriel Kay's A Song for Arbonne', Tor.com/Reactor (2010-08-06)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/provencal-fantasy-guy-gavriel-kays-a-song-for-arbonne/",
   "quote": "a trajectory away from magic"
  },
  {
   "feature": "ninety-percent familiar base",
   "claim": "Palmatier argues Kay's worlds are essentially ninety percent familiar, altered through everyday rituals and lore (riselka) so each change informs the whole.",
   "source": "Joshua Palmatier, 'Altering the Familiar: Tigana by Guy Gavriel Kay', Tor.com/Reactor (2016-07-06)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/altering-the-familiar-tigana-by-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "essentially 90 percent familiar"
  },
  {
   "feature": "impressionistic language",
   "claim": "Palmatier calls Kay's writing evocative, even impressionistic in places, transporting through simple word choices and turns of phrase.",
   "source": "Joshua Palmatier, 'Altering the Familiar: Tigana by Guy Gavriel Kay', Tor.com/Reactor (2016-07-06)",
   "url": "https://reactormag.com/altering-the-familiar-tigana-by-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "evocative, even impressionistic in places"
  },
  {
   "feature": "prolepsis",
   "claim": "Hood says Kay relies heavily on prolepsis, revealing outcomes first so reading becomes a countdown scanning every sentence for tripwires of fate.",
   "source": "J.S. Hood, 'The Lions of Al-Rassan: A Masterclass in Weaving the Slow Burn', LitStack (2026-05-30)",
   "url": "https://www.litstack.com/the-lions-of-al-rassan-by-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "Kay relies heavily on prolepsis"
  },
  {
   "feature": "obstructed climactic view",
   "claim": "Hood notes the final duel is seen through Jehane's eyes, obstructed by a veil of sunlight showing only silhouettes, identity withheld to the end.",
   "source": "J.S. Hood, 'The Lions of Al-Rassan: A Masterclass in Weaving the Slow Burn', LitStack (2026-05-30)",
   "url": "https://www.litstack.com/the-lions-of-al-rassan-by-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "obstructed by a veil of sunlight"
  },
  {
   "feature": "momentum by subtleties",
   "claim": "Hood says the novel's momentum is driven by subtleties: a shared look, a double-meaning line of poetry, a change of stance.",
   "source": "J.S. Hood, 'The Lions of Al-Rassan: A Masterclass in Weaving the Slow Burn', LitStack (2026-05-30)",
   "url": "https://www.litstack.com/the-lions-of-al-rassan-by-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": "The novel’s momentum is driven by subtleties"
  },
  {
   "feature": "telling not showing (criticism)",
   "claim": "Hobbs (Globe and Mail, via Wikipedia) finds Kay's prose an obstacle, describing a character as unconventional rather than enacting it, with bothersome Tolkien-like sentence structures.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: Children of Earth and Sky (Reception section citing Hobbs, Wolfe, Kirkus, Capossere/Jones, Alexander)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Children_of_Earth_and_Sky",
   "quote": "stop finding bothersome sentence structures"
  },
  {
   "feature": "restrained supernatural",
   "claim": "Wolfe and Kirkus (via Wikipedia) describe Kay's supernatural as intentionally restrained, a light touch with the fantasy.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: Children of Earth and Sky (Reception section citing Hobbs, Wolfe, Kirkus, Capossere/Jones, Alexander)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Children_of_Earth_and_Sky",
   "quote": "light touch with the fantasy"
  },
  {
   "feature": "macro-to-micro scaling",
   "claim": "Capossere (via Wikipedia) says the story shifts seamlessly from macro to micro, scaling down epic events so history becomes humanized.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: Children of Earth and Sky (Reception section citing Hobbs, Wolfe, Kirkus, Capossere/Jones, Alexander)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Children_of_Earth_and_Sky",
   "quote": "scaling down epic events so that history becomes humanized"
  },
  {
   "feature": "slow pace, journeys",
   "claim": "Alexander (via Wikipedia) says Kay never races to a destination; the joy is in the journeys; the slow pace is typical.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: Children of Earth and Sky (Reception section citing Hobbs, Wolfe, Kirkus, Capossere/Jones, Alexander)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Children_of_Earth_and_Sky",
   "quote": "joy of his novels is invariably in the journeys"
  },
  {
   "feature": "carried-away prose (criticism)",
   "claim": "Kirkus (via Wikipedia) notes writing that sometimes gets carried away with itself among Kay's usual elements.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: A Brightness Long Ago (Reception citing Domise, Wolfe, Capossere/Jones, Alexander, Kirkus)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Brightness_Long_Ago",
   "quote": "that sometimes gets carried away with itself"
  },
  {
   "feature": "fringe characters given depth",
   "claim": "Alexander (via Wikipedia) praises fringe characters created with depth, tenderness and intelligence, the insignificant given significance.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: A Brightness Long Ago (Reception citing Domise, Wolfe, Capossere/Jones, Alexander, Kirkus)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Brightness_Long_Ago",
   "quote": "depth and tenderness and intelligence"
  },
  {
   "feature": "gift with language",
   "claim": "De Lint (via Wikipedia) credits small telling historical insights and Kay's sheer gift with language.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: The Sarantine Mosaic (Reception citing Charles de Lint; Further reading Borowska-Szerszun)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sarantine_Mosaic",
   "quote": "Kay’s sheer gift with language"
  },
  {
   "feature": "appealing minor characters",
   "claim": "Dirda (via Wikipedia) notes Under Heaven is leavened with appealing minor characters.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: Under Heaven (novel) (author's letter; Reception citing Wiersema, Dirda)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Under_Heaven_(novel)",
   "quote": "appealing minor characters"
  },
  {
   "feature": "right to telescope events",
   "claim": "Kay's author's letter for Under Heaven (quoted on Wikipedia) reserves the right to change or telescope events so a history-knowing reader still cannot predict the story.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: Under Heaven (novel) (author's letter; Reception citing Wiersema, Dirda)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Under_Heaven_(novel)",
   "quote": "I reserve the right to change, or telescope events"
  },
  {
   "feature": "many characters slow the tale (criticism)",
   "claim": "Dusmann (via Wikipedia) says the in-depth examination of so many characters slows The Last Light of the Sun with little left to wonder about.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: The Last Light of the Sun (Reception citing Hromic, Dusmann)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_Light_of_the_Sun",
   "quote": "in-depth examination of so many characters slows the tale down"
  },
  {
   "feature": "glimpsed threads of a larger tapestry",
   "claim": "Hromic (via Wikipedia) describes the usual sense of far more in the background than told, glimpsing a few shining threads in a larger tapestry.",
   "source": "Wikipedia: The Last Light of the Sun (Reception citing Hromic, Dusmann)",
   "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_Light_of_the_Sun",
   "quote": "glimpsing a few shining threads in a larger tapestry"
  },
  {
   "feature": "weak magic keyed to romance sources",
   "claim": "Ringel notes magic in Arbonne is weak, no wizard's duels, in keeping with the scarcity of pagan marvels in real medieval romances.",
   "source": "Faye Ringel, 'Kay's Provence: From Arbonne to Ysabel' (ICFA 2009 / Anticipation 2009 paper)",
   "url": "https://brightweavings.com/kaysprovence/",
   "quote": "there are no wizard’s duels"
  },
  {
   "feature": "palimpsest of time",
   "claim": "Ringel reads Arbonne and Ysabel as portraying Provence as a palimpsest where older history bleeds through into the present.",
   "source": "Faye Ringel, 'Kay's Provence: From Arbonne to Ysabel' (ICFA 2009 / Anticipation 2009 paper)",
   "url": "https://brightweavings.com/kaysprovence/",
   "quote": "a palimpsest of time and space"
  },
  {
   "feature": "centuries collapsed (Bethune)",
   "claim": "Ringel quotes Bethune that events separated by centuries are often collapsed into a single era while only the cultural milieu is kept.",
   "source": "Faye Ringel, 'Kay's Provence: From Arbonne to Ysabel' (ICFA 2009 / Anticipation 2009 paper)",
   "url": "https://brightweavings.com/kaysprovence/",
   "quote": "often collapsed into a single era"
  },
  {
   "feature": "troubadour poems transformed",
   "claim": "Ringel shows Kay transforming troubadour poems (Peire Vidal, Bernart de Ventadorn) into the in-world song of the title.",
   "source": "Faye Ringel, 'Kay's Provence: From Arbonne to Ysabel' (ICFA 2009 / Anticipation 2009 paper)",
   "url": "https://brightweavings.com/kaysprovence/",
   "quote": "transforms these and other troubadour poems"
  },
  {
   "feature": "elegiac tone and consequence",
   "claim": "Tarantino's essay characterises Kay's writing as elegiac and gently melancholy, turning on memory, continuity and the consequences of conduct (read via summary; quote withheld).",
   "source": "Bob Tarantino, 'The fantastic fiction of Guy Gavriel Kay', C2C Journal (2016-09-07)",
   "url": "https://c2cjournal.ca/2016/09/the-fantastic-fiction-of-guy-gavriel-kay/",
   "quote": ""
  }
